 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing is $O(N \cdot P)$ where $N$ is the number of cells and $P$ is the number of planes per axis [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(S)$ where $S$ is the number of slices, dominated by rasterization and blending [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for polygons for all three axes, which can be significant for large datasets [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Very fast on modern GPUs due to reliance on rasterization hardware [S2][S3, unofficial]. It is an object-space approach, efficient for modern graphics processors, making it the choice for interactivity [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles unstructured and curvilinear grids well [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. Allows for progressive refinement [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. Leverages hardware acceleration [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires significant preprocessing time [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. Polygon count can be high [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. Small cells may be missed without anti-aliasing [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. View-dependent axis selection may cause popping if not handled smoothly [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization of unstructured or curvilinear grid data [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-quality perspective rendering with accurate ray lengths is required without per-pixel corrections [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. When the dataset is too large to store all slice polygons in memory [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing is $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of cells and $P$ is the number of planes per axis, as each cell is sliced against all planes [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(M)$, where $M$ is the number of generated polygons, dominated by rasterization and blending [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$ to store the polygon arrays for all planes [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** The View-Oriented Planes Technique (a variant) with 400 planes on a $181 \times 217 \times 181$ volume significantly reduced speed due to fragments and memory accesses [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Leverages hardware rasterization and blending; supports progressive refinement; handles unstructured grids [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Preprocessing is expensive; small cells may be missed without splatting; perspective view requires opacity corrections [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Interactive visualization of unstructured grids where view direction is known or changes slowly; when hardware blending is available [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When high-quality perspective rendering with varying ray lengths is critical without post-correction; when memory is constrained due to polygon storage [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Plane Slicing was proposed by Yagel et al. [S1, S2][S3, unofficial] and described in detail in [15] of the source papers [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It is a method for volume rendering for curvilinear and unstructured grids [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. The method was parallelized for large datasets [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It is used in scientific visualization [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
+Plane Slicing was proposed by Yagel et al. [4] and detailed by Max et al. in the context of volume rendering for curvilinear and unstructured grids [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It was developed to address the challenges of rendering unstructured data using graphics hardware, leveraging the efficiency of polygon rasterization and alpha blending [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. The method has been parallelized for large datasets [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
 
